<h2>About Me</h2>
<p>
	My name is Amanda Sebring (she/her) and I am a Licensed Clinical Social Worker and clinical supervisor. 
	I primarily offer individual and family therapy to adolescents and young adults (approximately ages 14-30). 
	I specialize in the treatment of depression, anxiety, and trauma symptoms. Additionally, I have extensive 
	experience and am comfortable working with individuals who experience suicidal ideation or engage in 
	non-suicidal self-injury (frequently referred to as self-harm). My goal in therapy is to create a supportive 
	environment in which clients feel respected and understood, where they can work toward self-identified goals 
	and build a life worth living. 
</p>

<h3>Past Experience</h3>
<p>
	I have worked in the mental health field for over 13 years, supporting folks 
	across the lifespan. 
</p>
<p>
	I spent the early years of my career working with elderly individuals living 
	with dementia. I truly enjoyed these years, supporting residents with 
	activities of daily living, facilitating enriching activities, hearing stories about 
	people's lives, and reading residents some beautiful poetry that they wrote in their younger 
	years. I noticed that above all, family visits were the thing that elicited the most joy in residents. 
	This is the point of my career when I begin to develop the belief that healing and a sense of belonging 
	occurs in the context of relationships. 
</p>
<p>
	Throughout multiple years in this role, I supported multiple families through the process of hospice and the 
	passing of their loved ones. I think back on this time with profound gratitude, recognizing that this is 
	where my passion for working with families took root. 
</p>
<p>
	After earning a graduate degree, I returned to a residential treatment setting, this time as a Child and 
	Family Therapist. For years, I worked closely with adolescents and their families to improve communication 
	in the home, support parents with psychoeducation about their child's mental health challenges, engage in 
	collaborative safety planning, and worked toward one common goal - to support the youth and family with 
	being able to have the tools and resources necessary to safely return back to the community. Throughout 
	this time, I developed a strong skillset in working with clients individually, in group settings, and in 
	family therapy sessions. I also have valuable experience with case management and supporting folks with 
	accessing community-based resources. 
</p>

